* carry lockfile candidates on spells.Manifest and resolve a project's lockfiles up to the root; add magus query kind:package

Steps 1 and 2 of the dependency-nodes plan: the knowledge graph gains package
nodes sourced from a project's resolved lockfiles, so "what depends on X" can
answer for a third-party package and not only a workspace project.

Committed from the worktree it was written in, unchanged, so the work is on its
branch rather than living as 49 uncommitted files.

* regenerate after the two merges: hook manpage gains --observe and --transcript, graph gains the package kind

* serve the working diff and an annotated review over the console API

Two routes, split by cost. /api/v1/diff is an index read that returns the
unified patch in milliseconds; /api/v1/review loads the symbol shards and walks
a reverse closure. Folding them together would hold the whole diff behind the
slowest overlay for no reading benefit, so the console paints the patch first
and decorates it when the annotations land.

types.Review is a JOIN over answers the workspace already had - ClassifyFiles
for the declared-output role, impact.Compute/Enrich for reach and coverage - not
a new computation. SortForReview is the one definition of review order, so the
console, the CLI, and a Buzz advisor rank files the same way: generated output
last whatever its reach, then widest reach first, path only as a tiebreak.

Reach is the widest FileCount among a file's changed symbols, not their sum: a
file is as dangerous as its most-depended-on export, and summing would rank many
narrow symbols above one load-bearing API.

Verified against a running daemon on this changeset: magus.go ranks first at
reach 43, 62 percent covered.

* report a changed symbol's exposure: cross-project referents and module API

The semver-relevant question is not whether a symbol's text changed, it is who
can see it. Two ORTHOGONAL answers, because neither implies the other and
conflating them answers the wrong question.

ExternalProjects names the other workspace projects that reference the symbol -
who rebuilds. ModuleAPI says the symbol is exported from the module - who
outside the workspace can import it. Measured on this repository, every referent
of the root package sits in the root project, so cross-project exposure ALONE
reported the published SDK surface as internal; magus.go now reads public and
names Magus, Open, Close, Inspect.

exportedFromModule answers Go only, and deliberately: Go states export in the
language (initial capital) and unreachability in the path (an internal segment
the toolchain enforces), so the answer is a fact. Every other language returns
false, which reads as not-known-to-be-API and never as internal. Adding one
needs a rule the toolchain ENFORCES - TypeScript's export keyword does not
qualify, since what a package publishes is decided by its entry points.

Surface defaults to unknown and is only lowered to internal for a file the
symbol index covered: we-did-not-look must never render as we-found-nothing.

This stops short of claiming a break, and cannot honestly go further here.
Signature compatibility needs a base-side index magus does not have - the symbol
shards describe the working tree, not history - and language semantics magus
does not model. That belongs in a per-language spell op whose output joins this
same review.

* add the console Review surface: a virtualized hunk stream over the working diff

Virtualization is the feature, not an optimization. The scroll space is a spacer
sized to rows x ROW_HEIGHT and only the rows intersecting the viewport exist as
elements, so a ten-thousand-line diff costs what a hundred-line one does. Fixed
row height and no wrapping follow from that: a wrapped line is a variable-height
row and the constant-time position calculation collapses.

parse.ts reads git's extended unified format and is pure, so the same reader
serves this patch and a provider's later. rows.ts flattens to one addressable
row array and owns the split-view pairing, where a deletion run zips against the
addition run that follows it - emitting each line as it arrives would stack old
above new, which is the unified view in two columns.

Two parser bugs found by the tests rather than by reading: a trailing newline
was read as a content line and appended a phantom row to whatever hunk ended the
patch, and a bare patch's second file was swallowed because the open hunk was
not counted when deciding whether --- started a new file.

console-diff-* is a new area in PATTERNFLY.md's closed set, documented there:
PF has no diff component, and the row geometry is load-bearing scroll math.

BASELINE ONLY. It does not yet consume /api/v1/review, so none of the
annotations reach the screen, and its keys are a raw listener rather than the
console command registry.

* pair a person and an agent on one review session

The daemon holds ONE session per workspace; the console reads it over HTTP and
an agent reads it through the magus_review MCP tool. Three transports over one
object, because the daemon already multiplexes them over one workspace and three
privately-rebuilt reviews would be three diverging opinions of one changeset.
Sharing it is what lets an agent see where the person is looking and be useful
about it rather than narrating blindly.

Two capabilities are deliberately ABSENT from the agent surface.

It cannot move the cursor. suggest() enqueues a proposal the human accepts with
one key, and accepting is the only path to the viewport. An agent that could
scroll the reader's screen would make them stop trusting their own place in the
diff, and a lost place cannot be restored because it was in their head - which
is the guard's own rule, that you deny what cannot be undone and explain the
rest, read onto the review surface.

It cannot claim to be the human. Author is stamped from the transport the write
arrived on: the console route always stamps human, the MCP tool always agent,
and a payload that says otherwise is ignored. Verified against a live daemon - a
console comment carrying author=agent was recorded as human.

It also cannot mark a hunk read, quietly on purpose: read is a claim only the
reader can make.

State is split by lifetime. Coordination (cursor, comments, suggestions) lives
in memory, because outliving the conversation would resurrect stale suggestions
into a review nobody is having. Progress persists, keyed by hunk content digest
rather than by line number, so a mark survives a rebase that did not touch the
hunk - the failing of every viewed-checkbox that resets on force-push.

* make the console a client of the shared review session

The surface now reads what the workspace knows and spends the reader's
attention accordingly.

NOISE COLLAPSE. A declared target output is a machine's restatement of a change
made elsewhere, so generated files leave reading order entirely and fold into
one row. Measured on this branch's own diff: five changed files became three to
read plus two folded. They are also excluded from the progress denominator,
because "40 files left" is a lie when thirty of them are files a target rewrote.

CONSEQUENCE ORDER. The server already ranks by what a change can break
(SortForReview) and the stream and sidebar both follow it, rather than
re-implementing an order in the browser that would drift from the CLI's.

THE BLAST RAIL. Per file: how many files reference its widest changed symbol,
whether it is public surface (naming the exported symbols), and measured
coverage. Evidence, never verdicts - and a file nobody measured renders NO
coverage chip, because a 0% badge on unmeasured code is the fastest way to make
the whole rail untrustworthy.

VIEWED SURVIVES A REBASE. Marks are keyed to a hunk content digest shared
byte-for-byte with the Go side, pinned by one golden vector asserted in both
suites - drift there would silently desynchronize what the person marked read
from what an agent sees. Verified live: a mark held across a reload that changed
the diff around it.

Two bugs found by driving the real browser rather than by reading. Esc opened
the overview and then bubbled to an ancestor handler that closed it again, so it
appeared inert. And each surface is its own bundle, so lib/daemon's
adopt-this-origin flag is per-bundle state: without adopting it here, Review
reported no daemon on a console served by that very daemon until the reader had
visited the dashboard first.

* add magus review: the terminal client of the shared review session

Three transports over one annotation join - the console surface, this command,
and an agent through magus_review - so a person reviewing in a terminal and an
agent pairing with them see the same ranking rather than two tools' opinions of
one changeset.

It reviewed its own changeset while being written: 45 changed files became 6 to
read and 39 generated folded.

WorkingDiff now includes UNTRACKED files, which is not a detail: a brand-new
file is the thing a reviewer most wants to see, and every backend's dirty-diff
is tree-against-index, so a new file was invisible. The untracked half is
composed here rather than by widening DirtyDiff, whose tree-against-index
meaning drift gates depend on. Untracked paths come from two capabilities the
backends already expose - DirtyFiles for everything dirty, TrackedFiles for
which of those the VCS knows - so it stays backend-agnostic instead of learning
git's porcelain column format.

One bug worth naming, found by noticing a file I had just written was absent
from its own review. The two halves are concatenated, and a tracked diff whose
last line is unterminated - exactly what a diff ending in "No newline at end of
file" produces - glued the first synthesized header onto it. That header then
stopped starting a line, every reader missed it, and precisely one new file
vanished while the rest appeared normally. Nothing errored. The test asserts the
header BEGINS a line, because Contains would have passed the whole time.

* make the console command registry actually process-wide

Its comment already claimed one process-wide map; that was false across the one
boundary that matters. Each surface is its own esbuild entry point loaded with a
runtime import(), so every bundle inlined a private copy of the module and got a
private Map. A surface registering a command wrote it into a registry the
shell's command bar and Actions surface never read - the command worked under
the surface's own keydown handler and was invisible everywhere else.

Found by grepping the built bundles rather than the source: a surface's command
ids appeared in its own bundle and in no other. Verified fixed in a live
console - the Review surface's twelve commands now show up in the shell's
registry of 41.

Hanging it off globalThis is the smallest thing that makes the existing claim
true. Anything genuinely shared between the shell and a surface has to cross
that boundary through storage or a global; getLiveToken already works for
exactly this reason, because it reads sessionStorage rather than a module
variable.

* gofmt the import block and the untracked-diff tests

* silence three deliberate degradations and the unreachable hasher error

Each nilerr site is a documented choice, not an oversight: a workspace with no
VCS has nothing to review rather than a failure, an unreadable scratch file must
not make the whole changeset unreadable, and a blast radius that cannot be
walked degrades to a Note so the absence stays visible. The Note is the part
that matters - it is what keeps 'not measured' from reading as 'nothing depends
on this'.

hash.Hash.Write is documented never to return an error, so the returns are
discarded explicitly. Handling a branch that cannot be taken would be
untestable error handling in a hot path.

* show the conversation in the diff: comments render inline and can be written from the console

The pairing substrate was real to agents and invisible to people - the store,
the route, the MCP tool and the authorship stamping all worked, and a human
sitting in the Review surface still could not see what an agent had said. That
is backwards for a feature whose whole argument is human-first.

A comment is a ROW, directly under the hunk it annotates, inside the
virtualizer's geometry. A remark read three inches from the code it is about is
a remark the reader has to hold in their head, which is the thing this surface
exists to stop asking of them. Its anchor is the hunk's index WITHIN its file,
not a global row index - a global one would slide the comment onto a different
hunk the moment the generated group folded, and it is also the coordinate an
agent speaks in, so a remark written in the console and one written over MCP
land in the same place.

c opens a composer, r resolves. The composer is pinned to the scroll container
rather than inserted into the virtualized window, because that window is
replaced wholesale on every scroll frame and a composer living in it would be
destroyed mid-sentence. Its keydown stops propagation so a bare v while typing
is the letter v.

Verified as an actual conversation against a live daemon: a comment typed in the
browser was stamped human and rendered, an agent read it over MCP and replied,
and both turns showed up on the hunk labelled YOU and AGENT.

Two visual corrections from looking at it rather than reasoning about it. The
body font is taller than the mono font at the same nominal size, so comment text
overflowed the fixed row box - the text had to fit the row, since the
virtualizer's geometry is not negotiable. And the highlight token is a saturated
yellow that dominated dark mode and dropped the text's contrast while fighting
the diff for attention; the coloured left border already says who is speaking,
so the fill only has to separate the row from the code.

* answer the question a diff cannot: is this file being rewritten over and over

The churn lenses join the review, so a reader sees at review time that a file is
among the workspace's most-changed and that its project's churn is accelerating.
That is the circle you normally only notice in hindsight, after the fifth visit
- and review is the one moment somebody is already looking at the file and could
still decide to fix the cause instead of the symptom.

AttachChurn takes its lens data as an argument rather than computing it, because
the two halves have different costs and different freshness needs. The
annotations are cheap and must be current; the history lenses are a bounded
git-log scan the daemon already keeps warm for the dashboard. So the daemon
passes its cached scan and the CLI pays for a fresh one, and there is still a
single definition of how the numbers land on a file.

InsightOptions.Files had to be set on the daemon's cached scan. Without it the
lens ranks PROJECTS and the per-file list is empty, so every file silently
reported no churn - it reuses the same scan, so the cost is formatting a list
the data was already there for.

Two display decisions that are not the same as the data. A rank is honest at any
depth and meaningless to show at any depth: 'hotspot #1278' tells a reader
nothing except that a ranking exists, and a field that is usually noise is a
field they learn to skip - which then costs them the one time it says #3. So the
rank renders only inside a cutoff while the commit count, which stands on its
own, always does. And rising requires BOTH a notable rank and an accelerating
project, because either alone is ordinary: plenty of files are hot for being
big, and plenty of projects accelerate for good reasons.

Nil churn stays nil rather than becoming a confident zero - 'nobody measured'
and 'this file is quiet' are different facts.

* let Notes and Activity find the daemon that is serving them

Same per-bundle state bug the Review surface hit. Each surface is its own
esbuild entry point loaded with a runtime import(), so lib/daemon's
adopt-this-origin flag is private to each bundle - the shell setting it does not
make it true inside a surface, and daemonAttach then returns null on a console
served by that very daemon.

Both surfaces appeared to work because they fall back to the host the DASHBOARD
persists to localStorage, so they were fine for anyone who had opened the
dashboard first and broken for anyone who had not.

* reconstruct the story behind a changed file from the agent trail

A guard hook observes every path an agent reaches, so magus can answer what an
agent was LOOKING AT immediately before it wrote something - the closest thing
to the author's reasoning any tool can recover without asking them. A diff shows
what changed; this shows what the change was made in response to, and it is a
sentence no forge can say.

The transcript is a POINTER and magus never opens it. The trail stays a record
of paths and timings, so a whole session's reach is cheap to carry while the
expensive and sensitive detail stays where the host already put it.

Two ordering decisions the tests pin. The walk is OLDEST first, so a write can
snapshot what preceded it - a path reached after the edit must not retroactively
become its explanation. And the file written is dropped from its own read list,
because an agent almost always reads a file before editing it and reporting that
as context crowds out the paths that actually explain the edit.

The events are REVERSED rather than sorted by timestamp. The events file is
append-only, so its order is the chronology; Ts is a lossy shadow stamped in
whole milliseconds. A burst of observations - an agent reading several files then
writing one - lands on identical timestamps, and a stable sort over ties keeps
whatever order it was handed. ReadRecent hands back newest-first, so sorting by
Ts walked the whole thing backwards and every read looked like it happened after
the write it explained.

Paths are relativized against the workspace root. Hooks record the path exactly
as the host supplied it, which is ABSOLUTE for every host observed so far, while
a review speaks workspace-relative; without this the two vocabularies never meet
and every file reports no history, which is indistinguishable from no hook being
wired.

* make the observe hook prefer the workspace's own magus, and bump every template to 5

The observe template resolved magus from PATH only, unlike its two siblings. In
magus's own repository the PATH copy is an older release that does not know
--observe at all: it rejects the flag, prints usage to a stream the script
discards, and exits non-zero into a swallowed failure. So the observation was
never recorded - silently, forever, with correct wiring and a green doctor.

Measured here: the trail held 3252 agent events and not one file read, while
.claude/settings.json had the Read matcher wired the whole time. This file needs
the ./magus preference MORE than the guards do, because it is silent by design -
a guard that cannot run at least leaves a verdict missing, and this just leaves
a gap nobody can see.

The version marker moves to 5 across every template at once, which is what the
constant and its test exist to force: a file left behind would claim a version
whose behavior it does not have, and a wrong answer is worse than a missing one.

* show the story in the diff: who wrote this file and what they were reading

The replay join existed and only the CLI rendered it. A story row now sits under
each file heading: the agent that wrote it, the paths it had read first, and a
pointer to the host's own transcript.

Under the FILE heading rather than a hunk, because that is the granularity the
trail records - an agent wrote the file, and what it had read applies to the edit
as a whole. Pinning it to one hunk would claim a precision the data does not
have.

Dimmer than a comment, and dotted rather than solid on the left. A comment is
addressed to the reader; this is context they may want. It has to be findable
without competing with the code or with an actual remark.

With no reads recorded the sentence stops at the author rather than inventing a
reason for the change.

* pass the transcript from the write and command guards, not just observe

Only the observe template sent --transcript. But a Touch is built from WRITE
events, so the replay could always name the agent and never link to its log -
which is the half that lets a reader go read what was actually said. The pointer
existed end to end and was never populated by the hook that mattered.

Both guards now select HOST_TRANSCRIPT_PATH beside the session id and pass it
with the rest of the attribution, so it rides the same best-effort retry: an
older binary that rejects the flag falls back to the unattributed call rather
than losing the verdict.

Verified by driving the path guard with a synthetic event and reading the blob
it wrote: session and transcript both recorded.

Version 6 across every template, which is what the constant forces - a file left
at 5 would claim behavior it does not have.

* expose magus\review to Buzz and add the api-surface advisor

The last stated requirement from the original brief: CI-provider agnostic, in
Buzz, outside the Go codebase - the same shape the thirteen advisors beside it
already take. Only action.yml is GitHub-shaped, so another provider needs no Go
change.

The advisor reports API surface: symbols reachable outside the project that
defines them, or exported from the module. That is the version-bump
conversation, and it is the one a reviewer usually has too late - after a
release, from a consumer's bug report.

It states EVIDENCE and never a verdict, and the file says why at length: magus
cannot decide a break without a base-side index it does not keep and language
semantics it does not model, and a breaking-change warning nobody trusts is
worse than none. A test asserts the body never contains the word breaking.

magus\review shells out to  rather than reimplementing the join,
which is the point of exposing it: the advisor, the console, and the CLI now
rank files by one definition of review order, and changing it reaches all three.

Generated output is skipped even when it looks public - a regenerated binding
file exports plenty, and none of it is a decision anyone made in the pull
request.

Three gates taught me the surface's rules, and each is a good rule: a
Buzz-visible object must be declared in boundaryTypes, the dry-run playground
needs a stub with the result shape so field access resolves in a dry run, and
the module docs are generated. Buzz mirrors are camelCase, not the JSON
snake_case.

The advisor's pure functions take PRIMITIVES so they are testable: a host object
cannot be constructed inside a test block, so a function taking one could only
be exercised against a live workspace - which is exactly the code most worth
pinning.

* stop the review claiming a ranking it does not have: reach becomes nil when unmeasured and the surface says UNRANKED before the list; reject a positional ref instead of swallowing it; keep raw command lines out of the trail after one leaked a live token; count a project as edited only when a person changed a source in it; read a file with no history sooner, not last

* rename the review surface to diff: magus diff, magus_diff over MCP, types.Diff, internal/diff, and magus\diff in Buzz; the annotated changeset takes /api/v1/diff while the raw patch moves to /api/v1/diff/patch, because review promised a forge verdict this tool does not carry

* read a patch from stdin or a file, watch the tree, and link every path; fire the UNRANKED banner on the real signal (a graph loads fine with no symbol shards in it, so gating on a non-nil graph reported unmeasured reach as a measured zero); record agent_name on a comment; say which command explains a fold; state the hotspot cutoff

* make the virtualized grid legible to assistive tech: aria-rowcount carries the true total and aria-rowindex the absolute position, so a screen reader stops reporting the overscan window as the whole diff; announce add and delete in words rather than by colour and a glyph; announce a suggestion once through a polite live region; honour prefers-reduced-motion in both the stylesheet and scrollTo

* highlight which part of a changed line changed: pair a run of deleted lines with the added run that follows and emphasise the span between the common prefix and suffix, on token boundaries so a rename marks the whole name; unequal runs are left alone because pairing across them invents a correspondence the patch does not contain

* stop shipping SCIP locals in the annotated diff: a symbol nothing references, that leaves neither its project nor the module, cannot change how the change is read, and on a real file those were about two thirds of what every MCP client was served; a newly added export has no referents yet and is kept

* add a doctor check for whether the agent observer is actually recording: a hook that is wired but writing nothing is indistinguishable from an agent that read nothing and from a human, so the diff's strongest claim degrades in silence; fail on commands with no reads, advise when reads are too sparse to explain a change, and stay quiet below a sample worth judging

* record what landed on the diff surface overnight, the endpoint split the rename forced, the HasSymbols trap that kept the UNRANKED banner silent, and the six items plus the Buzz-WASM thread that were not reached

* regenerate the knowledge graph for the handoff note

* point the godoc at the renamed symbols and drop a nolint the rename made unnecessary

* record the gate result in the handoff: two real findings fixed, the remainder leaking from a sibling worktree

* stop serving an agent a changeset that stopped existing: the session carries the digest of the patch it was computed from, and op=state recomputes when the tree has moved instead of replaying whatever a browser last attached; ship the patch and its hunk coordinates so a comment can cite an index rather than guess one, and refuse a path or hunk the change does not contain

* record the agent-half fixes and the cross-language digest pin in the handoff

* rename the console surface the rename missed: the tab and its title still said Review

* give the diff back its colour: the add and delete rules named PatternFly status BACKGROUND tokens that do not exist, so every row resolved to transparent and a diff rendered as undifferentiated text; tint from the status colour tokens that do exist, accent each row with an inset bar the fixed row height can afford, and put the sidebar and toolbar on the secondary surface so the panels stop reading as one sheet; redirect a bare /console/<surface> to the trailing-slash form the shell's relative base requires

* give the diff syntax colour, which it never had: a small bundled tokenizer, because a strict CSP means highlighting is bundled or absent and the docs site's highlight.js and CodeMirror are build-only deps of a separate project that owns a separate bundle; a diff line is a fragment so anything ambiguous stays plain, but an unclosed block comment and a leading-star continuation are the shapes this codebase is made of and both are read; the palette is the docs site's own syn-* values so two surfaces do not disagree about what a keyword looks like

* stop telling a reader their tree is clean when the reader is what failed: the clean-tree state now says nothing to read, and a patch this parser could not handle says so instead of borrowing the empty state's words

* add a Sapling backend and a cross-backend parity suite; hg and jj both returned cwd-relative paths where git returns workspace-relative, and hg and sl merge hang without an explicit tool, so the suite pins the behaviour every backend has to share rather than the behaviour git happens to have

