Each of the three rejected a program the upstream compiler accepts, measured
against ~/Repos/buzz/zig-out/bin/buzz rather than reasoned about:

- var-not-assigned fired only for an IdentExpr target, so `digests[p] = h` and
  `point.x = 2` read as "never assigned". This is what stopped tools/drift.buzz
  from loading. markAssigned now walks the target to its root identifier, the
  same rule noteMutatingUse already applied to `.append`.
- DoStmt had no escaping-break guard, unlike While and For, so everything after
  a `do { break; } until (...)` was called dead.
- Shadowing was checked across a function boundary, so a nested closure could
  not reuse an outer name. fnScopeBase now stops the walk at the current
  function, and a nested block in the same function is still reported.

A fourth reported false positive was NOT one: upstream rejects a break inside a
try inside a while outright ("break is not allowed here"), so accepting it would
have been a permissive divergence. Left alone deliberately.

Six hermetic parity tests pin all of this, including both directions of the
mutability rule, since the upstream suites skip entirely without a checkout.

* Declare the mutated collections mut, as upstream requires

126 sites across 23 files were written `var files: [str] = mut [<str>]` and then
appended to. The annotation is what narrows the type, so the mutator is a type
error however the value was built - upstream rejects the same source in the same
words ("Method `append` requires mutable list"), so the rule was right and the
corpus was wrong. Widening the annotation is safe in the other direction: mut T
stays assignable to T, so no caller wanting the immutable form breaks.

Includes two sites the regex could not reach: gomod.buzz declared a map of
IMMUTABLE lists (`mut {str: [str]}`), and the two `var io = {}` scratch records
in the actions spell are filled by a callback rather than assigned, which is
what `final` is for.

* Take the dedup probe only for a regular file, so snapshotting a FIFO cannot block forever

snapshotOne opened the output twice: hashFile for a dedup fast-path, then
copyToFile. Opening a regular file twice is free. Opening a FIFO twice blocks
forever on the second open unless another writer happens to appear - there is no
timeout on a FIFO open and no diagnostic - so a declared output glob matching a
pipe hangs the build outright. The probe is now taken only when the file is
regular; everything else goes down the single-open path, where the
hash-while-copy already produces the authoritative name. A non-regular file has
no shard directory to aim at before that hash exists, so it stages in the CAS
root and is renamed into its shard afterwards, still one filesystem.

TestSnapshotOneHashMatchesWrittenBytes was itself the second half of the hazard.
It fed two write sessions to model "the content changed between the two reads",
but which reader a writer's open rendezvous with is pure scheduling, not the
determinism its comment claimed: under full-tree load the surviving open paired
wrongly and blocked, timing the package out at ten minutes. It now feeds exactly
one session, which is what its own comment says a fixed snapshotOne requires.

Measured: internal/cache went from FAIL at 601s (reproduced twice) to ok in 15s
under the same whole-tree run.

* Mark map keys and the collection under a foreach as reachable

markReachable had two holes, both in the dangerous direction: calling a live
object's collect() is unrecoverable, where missing a dead one only delays it.

- No tagIterState case. The collection a foreach walks is reachable ONLY through
  the iterator state while the loop runs - it is bound to no local and sits on no
  frame's env - so iterating three objects and calling gc\collect() in the body
  reclaimed the elements the loop had not reached yet.
- tagMap walked mo.Vals but not mo.keyVals, so an object used as a KEY was
  invisible to the mark while the map was still keyed on it.

The sweep had NO test of any kind, which is how both survived. The two added here
were confirmed to FAIL against the pre-fix collector before being kept, rather
than merely passing against the new one.

* Guard readStruct against use after collect; marshal bool struct fields in both directions

Two FFI defects, neither of which had any test.

readStruct decoded a pointer field by walking the ADDRESS stored in st.bytes.
collect() frees those addresses through st.owned but leaves st.bytes holding
them, so reading a struct back afterwards walked freed memory - observed
returning recycled heap, and able to fault outright past any recover, since
FreeFFI unpins. st.bytes cannot say whether its pointers are still live; st.freed
can, and ensurePinned already had the wording for it.

marshalStructArg's switch handled only tagInt and tagFloat, so a bool field was
never written: every bool reached C as the zero AllocFFI left, whatever the
script set. unmarshalStructArg then wrote IntValue over it, so a round trip
changed the field's TYPE as well as its value and `if (s.flag)` ran against an
int. The declared C type cannot tell a bool from a 1-byte integer, so the field's
existing tag is what says how to spell the result.

marshalStructArg/unmarshalStructArg are reachable in normal use only through a
real zdef'd call into a C library, which is why nothing covered them. The new
test drives them directly - the registries they read are plain package-level maps
- and was confirmed to fail against the pre-fix code on both directions.

* Keep the rest of the registry when a collector throws

On a collector error the registry was rebuilt from kept, which holds only the
instances the sweep had visited so far. Every collectable AFTER the failing one
was therefore deregistered outright, and its collect() would never run - on that
sweep or any later one. Only the failure is early; the remainder is untouched
work, not garbage, so the tail is spliced back on.

* Copy written bytes into a capacity-sized block; lay a union out as one; stop leaking the block on a failed marshal

Three FFI defects, all silent.

ensurePinned copied st.bytes into the pinned block only when no capacity had been
declared, so a buffer built with Buffer.init(capacity) and THEN written handed C
a block of zeros - the writes never arrived. Upstream's ffi.buzz uses the bare
Buffer.init() form, which is why conformance stayed green over it. The copy is
now unconditional, and the block sizes to whichever of capacity and written
length is larger, since taking capacity alone truncated a buffer written past it.

declaredAggregates recorded only {Size, Align}, which is the ANSWER for an
aggregate and not the RULE, so marshalStructArg - which lays the same name out
again to get field offsets - had nothing to tell a union from a struct and always
called StructLayoutWith. A union came out with struct offsets and a struct size,
disagreeing with the layout its own declaration had already published. NamedLayout
now carries IsUnion.

A failed marshalStructArg leaked its block: the function allocates early and
returns the block alongside a later failure, and the caller returned without
appending it to `pinned`, so the deferred FreeFFI never saw it. It is now pinned
before the error check. Separately, unmarshalStructArg called ReadScalar on a
field whose type is another declared aggregate, which CTypeLayout cannot resolve;
that error surfaced after the C function had already run and turned a good call
into a failure. Nested aggregates are now skipped, as pointer fields already were.

* Track the JIT tag expression rather than hardcoding an answer that only holds in the default build

TestJITBackendPresence asserted a backend is present on amd64/arm64 unconditionally,
but vm/jit_{amd64,arm64}.go carry `!buzz_safe && !buzz_unsafe` as well as the arch
clause: an alternate Value representation deliberately withholds the backends. Under
either tag the test therefore failed - "JITAvailable()=false but a backend is present
for this arch" - which is why the buzz_safe build was never gated in CI despite the
README claiming it. jitTagged carries the representation half of the expression, so
the assertion now tracks the tag algebra instead of an answer true only by default.

Folded from feat/musing-jang-14d74b, where it was uncommitted work (the two
jit_*_tagged files untracked). Verified green under no tags, -tags buzz_safe and
-tags buzz_unsafe, and confirmed to reproduce the documented failure without it.

* Glob the spell test gate; restore the root magusfile migration; declare the console raises

Three things the newly-built binary made visible, none of which any grep could.

buzz-test named three spell sources by hand, leaving spells/aws/s3-cache and
spells/onepassword under NO gate: neither is imported by a magusfile so neither
registers for doctor's spell-contract check, and cmd/magus-utils' built-in compile
reads spells/<dir>/spell.buzz one level deep (missing the nested aws/s3-cache) and
skips onepassword by name. Globbing means the next spell someone adds is covered by
existing. Folded from feat/admiring-meninsky-b896f1, where it was uncommitted; its
two generated files are deliberately not carried, since those get regenerated.

The root magusfile's two mutability sites were NOT migrated after all. They were
migrated, then destroyed by a `git checkout -- magusfile.buzz` I ran to revert a
bootstrap patch while the migration was still uncommitted. The check that was
supposed to catch it - "every changed .buzz line is a mut annotation" - could not,
because the file had stopped being changed at all. Absence is invisible to a diff
filter; only the checker sees it.

console's install() and lint() call proc\exec, which raises, but were never
re-declared after exec moved from os to proc - so the console project could not
load at all, and preflight() had to follow. Same fallout as the 15 call sites
already fixed in ccd7e92d9, missed because nothing had loaded the workspace since.

* Answer Exports() with the caller's own exports, not every module it imported

exportedNames accumulated chunk.Exports for every chunk the session ran, imports
included, and Exports() answered from it. That conflated two questions. The
VISIBILITY readers genuinely want the wide set - a name some flat import made
private stays visible if another module exported it - but every Exports() caller
asks about OWNERSHIP: what does this file declare? Target discovery, dry-run, and
spell function lookup all want the file's own exports.

So `import "lcov"` made lcov's percent and mergePercent look like exports of
console/magusfile.buzz, and target discovery then rejected them with MGS1008 for
taking no magus\Context. The console and libs/textsearch projects could not load
at all. rootExportedNames records only chunks run outside an import, which
collectImportPrivate already delimits exactly.

With that cleared the checker reached the docs libraries and found three more real
defects, none of which any grep would have: a mutating caller of splitPath (whose
other eight callers only read, so it takes its own copy rather than widening that
contract), a `var` never assigned, and two dead trailing returns after a
`while (true)` that has no break and exits only by return.

* Call fs\mkdirAll at the 22 sites that said fs\makeDirectory

BZZ1007 reports `module fs has no member "makeDirectory"`, and it is right: the
magus fs binding registers mkdirAll, while makeDirectory exists only as an entry
in std/buzz_stdlib.go's alias table. The calls resolved at run time and were never
declared, which is exactly what BZZ1007 exists to catch now that it is enforced.

Every call site passed a single path and mkdirAll's second parameter is optional,
so this is a rename with no shape change.

* Give Site's mutated collections a mut type and no default; call fs\listDir

Fourteen of Site's fields are mutated in place (docPages alone at 25 sites) but
were declared `name: [T] = mut [...]` - an immutable type holding a mutable value,
which is the shape the mutability rule rejects. Annotating them `mut` in place is
not available either: a mut field cannot carry a default, because the default
would be built once and shared by every instance, which is the hazard that rule
exists to prevent. So the default moves to the twelve `Site{}` construction sites,
where each instance allocates its own.

Only Site is affected. An earlier count of 57 construction sites was wrong - it
matched every object type in the file, not the one that owns these fields.

Separately, fs\list does not exist (the member is listDir); fifteen call sites said
otherwise and BZZ1007 is right to reject them, same as fs\makeDirectory before.

* Fix the docs engine's mutability, shadowing, raise and fs-member reports

Everything the checker found once a binary could load the workspace. Grouped
because they are five recurring classes, not five one-offs:

- Nested collections whose INNER type was immutable while the outer was mut:
  glossaryRefs, metaHistory and a local bySection are all mutated element-wise.
- A `var` that is only passed to a callee that fills it is never assigned in its
  own scope, so it is `final` (three in gates.buzz, matching the actions spell).
- Two same-function shadows in nav.buzz (`parts`, `rec`). Verified against the
  upstream compiler first, which rejects the shape in nearly the same words - and
  the USES were renamed with the declaration, since renaming only the binding
  would have silently rebound them to the outer one.
- fs\delete is not a member (it is Buzz's own stdlib spelling); removeAll is.
  That raises, so capture, loadMeta, axisReport and buildCommits declare !> any.
- walkGen's `acc` parameter took an immutable [str] and appended to it.

The std/buzz_stdlib.go table is NOT an alias mechanism, so none of the fs
renames could be fixed centrally: it is informational, and its right-hand values
are Buzz stdlib calls rather than magus members. Checked before renaming.

* Declare the raises the magusfiles and docs renderer always had, so the workspace loads

This is the last of what stood between the branch and a loadable workspace, and it
is all one class: a call that may raise, in a function that never said so. The
os\exec-to-proc\exec move made many more calls raising without touching a single
signature, and nothing had loaded the tree since, so none of it surfaced.

Twenty-odd functions across the root, evals and gopherbuzz magusfiles and the docs
renderer now declare !> any, propagated to their callers to a fixpoint. Two shapes
needed care beyond the obvious: a single-line `fun f() > str { ... }` body, and a
closure argument (os\withEnv's callback), which carries its own raise annotation
rather than inheriting the enclosing function's.

Also: render.buzz flat-imports engine/gates but a flat import does not RE-export,
so renderer\conventionsCheck was never resolvable. The docs magusfile imports
gates directly instead of laundering it through the renderer.

`magus ls` now reports all ten projects. It has not loaded on this branch since
the rename work landed.

* Regenerate the skill eval fixtures and refresh the merge-driver attributes

TestEvalFixturesMatchTheEmbeddedSkills has been the one red test in the tree all
of the previous session and this one. It was never a regression: the fixtures were
rendered by an older binary (agent-skill-version 31) while the source has been at
33, and nothing could regenerate them because no binary could load the workspace.
Both variants are re-rendered from this tree, so `go test ./...` is now fully green
at the magus root for the first time on this branch.

.gitattributes picks up two playground outputs (buzz.wasm, wasm_exec.js) that the
merge driver refresh found declared but unlisted. No duplicated entries.

* Regenerate the workspace indexes and graphs, 131 commits stale

gen/knowledge-graph.json was the top-ranked open item in the handoff and could not
be refreshed until a binary could load the workspace. Verified rather than assumed,
as that note insisted: the graph now carries 35 modules (it asserted 28), and
magus\targetGraph is gone. The three other names it flagged as stale are fine on
inspection - has_charm is a real current method, os\exec no longer appears at all,
and the mkdirall hits are the path std/examples/fs/mkdirall.buzz, not a member.
So `graph export --static` invalidation is not broken; the shard was simply never
regenerated.

Also removes a dead trailing return in the advice action's linkCodes, the same
`while (true)` with no break shape found three times in the docs libraries.

* Render a failure cause as one line per failure, with the target hops as a path

The cause line was unreadable on any run with more than one failure:

  cause: ctx.needs: build: ctx.needs: go-build: ctx.needs: ctx.needs: format: dprint exited 20 test: ctx.needs: advice-test: magus.buzz: buzz -t blast-radius.buzz exited with code 1 compress-cgo-test: go exited 1

Three separate problems, all now fixed:

- errors.Join renders its parts newline-separated and strings.Fields flattened
  them, so independent failures ran together into one sentence with no boundary -
  "dprint exited 20 test:" is two unrelated failures. One line each now, aligned
  under the first.
- The 240-rune excerpt applied to the CONCATENATION, so a long first failure could
  truncate the second and third away entirely. The budget is per cause now.
- Every ctx.needs hop re-wrapped the message, and a colon did double duty as both
  hop separator and message separator. The bare repeats are dropped and the hops
  render as a path, so the final colon marks exactly where the route ends:

  cause: build -> go-build -> format: dprint exited 20
         test -> advice-test -> magus.buzz: buzz -t blast-radius.buzz exited with code 1

A hop is recognized by shape (a bare token, no spaces); everything from the first
space-bearing segment is the message and is left untouched, so a message's own
colons are never mangled into fake hops.

This reverses a deliberate decision that flattened multi-line causes onto one line
for scannability. That premise held while a cause was always one error whose text
wrapped; it stopped holding once a cause routinely carries several independent
failures. The assertion is updated rather than removed, with the reasoning kept.

