Add registry (#42)

* Narrow -tags noselfupdate to the updater alone: the whole self dispatcher was written twice, once per tag, so a subcommand with nothing to do with updating still had to be added in both places and vanished from the stub if you forgot; move it to an untagged self.go and leave one function behind the tag

* Throw a status, url, and method from http rather than only a sentence: a Buzz caller could not tell a 404 from a 500 without substring-matching prose, which is the one part of an error nobody promises to keep stable; message is unchanged, so anything reading it still works

* Record in scope.md that the signed registry reverses the hosted-feed rejection, and say which of the four rejections it does not touch: the page argued a feed on our domain commits a project with no revenue to keeping an aggregator fresh, and that has not stopped being true - what changed is that a stale one now reports itself

* Extract the drop-in directory reader now there is a second user, and move connectors.d onto it: one file per entry earns its keep when an entry is meaningful alone, is added and removed one at a time, and would otherwise have separate writers contending on one file - and it does not earn it for a log, which is why run history stays a single file

* Add the registry: a signed file of release and end-of-life facts that only magus self refresh fetches and every other command reads from disk; sources are one file each in registry.d because where facts come from is a machine question, staleness is measured from the publisher's generated_at so a cron that quietly stopped reports itself rather than reading as forever-fresh, and MAGUS_OFFLINE is a named refusal rather than a silent skip

* Build the registry: magus-utils registry-build aggregates endoflife.date on its own daily workflow and signs with its own MAGUS_REGISTRY_KEY, because the release key is quarantined to one job on a v* tag and a job fed several hundred third-party responses is the last place it should be reachable; the fetch is a separate step from the transform so the transform is a pure function of files on disk, generated_at is derived from the newest input rather than the clock, and -verify rebuilds a published file from the digests it records

* Give the crypto module signing, with the algorithm as a declared SignAlgorithm enum rather than four ed25519-prefixed method names: adding a scheme is a case in the enum instead of four more methods, and the checker rejects a name magus does not implement at load rather than throwing halfway through a release job; the private key is named by environment variable and never crosses into Buzz, so it cannot be interpolated into a log

* Move the registry aggregator from Go into tools/registry.buzz, beside audit.buzz: which upstream to read and which of its fields matter is knowledge about the world, which docs/scope.md says belongs in a file anyone can change without cutting a release, not compiled into the binary; the workflow creates its branch and pull request through the gh CLI it already holds a token for rather than git plumbing, which also survives the shallow checkout

* Declare an enum a host method's signature names, rather than only one an object's field does: the types generator emits an enum into the mirror of the object that uses it, so an enum reachable only from an argument had nowhere to be declared and the extern referenced an undefined name - which parses, then fails at check time and silently drops the whole module's declarations; Ret gains Enum alongside Arg so the same holds on the way out

* Make an inferred enum case reach a host method, which took fixing two breaks in series: resolveImport collected a native module's declarations without ever binding an exported enum's VALUE, so the member access the compiler lowers a case to found null, and Str then returned empty for the enum value it was handed rather than the case's backing string; upstream Buzz has neither gap because its stdlib is source that gets executed, and vm.Value.EnumValue's own doc already named the second one as the failure a typed enum exists to prevent

* Add BenchmarkStr, since the enum unwrap sits on the path every string argument of every host call takes: measured 0 B/op and 0 allocs/op unchanged with every sample equal, which is the result that matters; the ns columns are inconclusive rather than good, because the baseline carried 103-121% variance and Str/absent - a path the change cannot reach, returning early on a short argument list - reported a significant -7.83%, so the harness was finding signal in unmodified code

* Regenerate the knowledge graph after rebasing onto main: the rebase conflicted only in this file, which is generated, so it is resolved by regenerating rather than by merging hunks

* Regenerate the Buzz module reference for crypto's signing methods: the page is drift-gated against the module descriptor, so adding sign, signFile, verify, and publicKey without it fails TestModuleDocsUpToDate

* Ship a built-in registry source, and implement the half of the pubkey rule that was missing: our domain verifies against a ring pinned in the binary while a third-party source must declare its own key, which is what lets a mirror of our file need no key of its own; a registry.d file of the same name replaces the built-in rather than adding a second, and enabled: false declines it once; the ring is empty until the keypair exists, so a refresh refuses BEFORE sending anything rather than asking a host for bytes it could not check

* Take the Fix off the registry doctor check: a Fix is what doctor --fix RUNS, so wiring self refresh there made a repair command fetch from our domain for someone who asked for neither, which is the consent rule the registry package doc states in the opposite direction; it also failed with no key pinned and took doctor --fix down with it over a state that is normal on a fresh install
