Add a toolchain view: describe tools, magus.tool.v1, and a console tile (#36)

* Add a toolchain view: magus describe tools, a magus.tool.v1 service, and a console tile; move the version-window check to run start so it follows the declaration rather than op dispatch

* Address the toolchain-view review: cache failed probes and key them by spell; keep the daemon's process-forking handler off the LAN share surface; record every project's version on a memo hit so a shared dir no longer skips the gate; split the four probe outcomes the CLI collapsed into one blank 'not found'

* Correct four package comments that describe code that is not there; name forecast's tags file after the function in it; move hostmem's doc-only file to doc.go and say why its test has no sibling

* Rename libs/orphanator to libs/testsprawl before its first tag: orphan means no owner everywhere else here, and this rule reports a test file that has one; fix a truncated zstd frame decoding as success on the cgo path; repoint three musttag entries at internal/json, and tag the provider-cache record the guard could then see

* Read the installer's asset name out of the signed manifest instead of rebuilding it: asking for static fetched the bare name, which is the dynamically linked build, and the other branch asked for a -cgo token no release has produced; accept the paperwork the archive now carries

* Probe the greatest version below the needed floor, not the previous minor's .0: a required_version set inside the previous series read as covered; flush the pending batch when the notifier closes errors first, which is the arm that actually fires; stop an unenumerated annotation level rendering as the quietest one

* Run internal/codec's tests under -tags liblzma,libzstd in ci: the ~470 cgo lines a release binary ships compiled in no test job, which is how a truncated zstd frame decoding as success reached main

* Feed the duration model from the record path so adaptive sharding stops packing uniform weights: run.go measured a duration nothing read, and the timestamp it left zero is what made merge-history discard every shard but the first; rename internal/codec to internal/compress

* Tighten the comments and user-facing text this branch added: cut restatement, prefer active voice, and keep each block to the why

* Match the installer's transport floor in the Go client: TLS 1.2 minimum, refuse a non-https source or redirect off loopback, and fetch each signature before the artifact it signs so a missing one fails cheap

* Read both asset-variant spellings when deriving a platform: releases through v0.3.0 wrote -static and -cgo, so every published asset resolved to a platform like darwin/arm64-static

* Check a redirect's scheme through requireHTTPS instead of restating it

* Return the origin's commit count from the fixture that built it, so the shallow-clone test stops walking 44 commits to re-measure a number it already knows; that walk is what failed in CI when a loose object could not be read back

* Backfill every release manifest with the size and digest its published assets actually carry, and add the v0.3.0 that was never written: the index they feed pinned nothing, because every one of those fields was the empty string; drop v0.1.0's magus-release.pem row, which names an asset that release never published

* Build and sign public/release/index.json in magus-utils instead of rendering it: only a tag build holds the signing key while the site rebuilds on every push to main, so a rendered index would keep putting its signature back to 404ing and self update back to exit 1; track the signed pair in place beside gen/install; stop cut appending a magus-release.pem no release publishes, and let it empty the [Unreleased] section the manifest owns from then on

* Call cut and release-index from the release that builds the artifacts, and add a manual workflow for the times the index has to change without a version being cut: a rotation, a corrected manifest, a yank; both open a pull request rather than pushing, because main's ruleset bypasses only for the admin role

* Trust a keyring rather than one release key, so a rotation stops skipping the people it is for: the documented chain only reached someone who installed the compatibility release, and SelectRelease takes the newest, so anyone a version behind jumped to a signature their binary had never been told to trust; a standby key ships long before it signs, the signed index carries key_id and a revoked list only believed from a non-revoked signer, and expires_at bounds replaying an old index that names no revocation, with a doctor check for the deadline it creates

* Store each connector token in its own file under connectors.d rather than as one entry in a connectors.json array: the cross-process lock, its retry loop, and its stale-lock steal heuristic existed only to make a read-modify-write safe, and there is no longer one to make safe - a create writes a temp file and hard-links it into place, which publishes atomically and fails EEXIST on a duplicate name, and a revoke is an unlink; migrate the old file once, and validate the name now that it is a path component

* Point the docs-site section at magus run serve, which is the target that actually shows you the change: generate:rw renders and then says nothing about how to look at it, and serve hosts both gen/ trees with the Pages layout and re-renders on edit
