Ci shard resource trace (#26)

* trace runner headroom during a shard so the next silent death explains itself; a shard that ends CANCELLED with 'the runner has received a shutdown signal' takes every later step with it, so an artifact upload can never collect anything and magus never reaches the summary it would have printed - the live log is the only channel that survives, which is why this samples to stdout from inside the step that runs magus rather than from one of its own

* carry a target's peak resident memory from the exec that measured it to the record that could act on it; the kernel already counted it and ExecResult.MaxRSSBytes already held it, but nothing outside that package consumed it, so the shard planner - the one caller that decides what may run alongside what - could not see the number that decides it; a context collector takes the MAXIMUM rather than the sum, because a target that forks a compiler then a linker peaked in whichever one peaked and summing describes a machine that never existed, and reports zero as UNKNOWN rather than as free

* separate recording a run's outcome from retrying it; the history the shard forecaster reads was only ever written for targets that opted into volatility retry, so a workspace where none do - this one - recorded nothing and the forecaster predicted a flat default for every project, unable to tell a heavy one from a light one; the runtime is now installed whenever history is enabled and carries the retry policy as a flag Decide honours, so a target that never asked for a retry still never gets one

* give the shard planner a memory budget it can only ever split on; the packer chose shard counts entirely in milliseconds, so two projects that together exceed a runner scored as a cheap consolidation and the job came back cancelled with no diagnostics; a shard's figure sums its projects because magus schedules them concurrently, unlike the processes inside one target which run in sequence, and a project with no recorded peak contributes nothing so an unmeasured workspace plans exactly as it did before

* derive the shard memory budget from the machine instead of asking for it; a config key defaulting to off would protect nobody while implying it did, and the number it wanted is a fraction of a runner's RAM nobody can see from a magusfile; three quarters of the host's memory leaves the agent, caches and toolchains their share, and a host magus cannot measure keeps planning exactly as before rather than on a guess

* cut the rescanning string loops out of the docs render and gate them out for good; str.replace substitutes only the first occurrence, so replace-all was written as a loop that copies the whole string once per hit, and on the default VM build every copy is interned for the life of the process - measured 5806MB to 4259MB peak on docs ci; collapsing runs needed a different shape than replace-all, and one chain was also only ever replacing the first newline of a page

* stop the advisory gate failing over patches nothing can install, fix an annotate step that never annotated, and lift the shard ceiling the memory constraint had nowhere to raise; the cooling check accepted any version above the patched floor, so nanoid's advisory matched majors 4 through 6 that cleared the ten-day window while the only reachable fix sat four days old behind stylelint's postcss, and a major bump is deferred permanently because widening a dependent's range is a human change rather than something actionable today

* commit the routing index my own new files moved, and stop the buzz-pattern gate tripping nilerr; a walk that skips an unreadable subtree returns nil deliberately, which is the one thing that linter cannot tell from swallowing a real error

* pin the forecaster tests' memory budget instead of deriving it from the host; a bare Forecaster reads this machine's RAM, so the same assertion meant one thing on a workstation and another on a 16GB runner, which is exactly how these passed locally and failed in CI

* stop md-generate replaying a MAGUS.md built before the change that moved it; its output is `describe graph -o markdown`, a function of the whole knowledge graph, while its only keyable inputs are the magusfiles - so any other edit leaves the key unmoved and a cache hit reverts the committed index, which the drift gate then reports as drift; this is why the gate failed only in CI, where the shared cache is restored, and passed on any checkout with nothing to hit

* regenerate the graph for the source lines my own magusfile comment moved

* name the files that drifted instead of listing the whole generated set; this gate is inert in a worktree, so the only place it fires is CI, where a message that says one of nine things moved costs a push per guess

* print the diff, not just the names, when a docs regeneration drifts; this gate is inert in a worktree so CI is the only place it fires, and a filename alone still leaves the reader guessing why a file they regenerated locally came out different on a runner

* fix the drift gate's own diagnostic: vcs\name is a property not a function, vcs\cmd needs its opts labeled, and there is no quiet option so the captured stdout was printing the diff a second time; print a lead line and let the command stream

* print the drift diff through magus\info rather than the command's own stdout; a tool's streamed output lands in the run log and the console keeps only likely diagnostics, which dropped the diff entirely, and bound it so a one-line generated JSON cannot bury a small markdown drift

* stop documenting vcs.name as a method; it is a field resolved once at registration, and the four descriptor doc-strings that wrote it with call parens are the source the docs site, the buzz reference and the editor hovers all render from, so the surface taught a call that fails at runtime with 'str is not callable'

* make vcs.name and vcs.base methods; a Module Field generates no extern declaration because Buzz has syntax for `extern fun` and none for an extern value, so the checker could not type either one and a magusfile calling vcs\name() compiled clean and died at runtime in a branch only CI reaches; gate the Field concept shut and carry the stale boundary_list regeneration

* stop capturing a live daemon token into committed documentation; magus-examples runs `magus explain` for real and its Graph Explorer deep-link carries the auth token, which auth.Load reads from the state dir with or without a running daemon, so the page embedded whichever token last regenerated it and the docs site published it; point the capture's XDG_STATE_HOME into its own fixture and gate the pattern

* print the diff in the root drift gate too, matching the docs one; a filename was not enough twice running, and the drift it finally exposed there was a daemon auth token captured into an example, which no list of names could have shown

* regenerate every project's MAGUS.md for the two new vcs methods; each project embeds a workspace-wide node-count table, so converting vcs.name and vcs.base from fields moved method 167 to 169 everywhere, and a per-project generate:rw fixes only the project it names

* give nightly a concurrency group so a hand dispatch supersedes the cron run instead of racing it; ci and release already cancel in progress and cd deliberately does not, leaving nightly the only workflow where two runs could overlap and skill-evals is the expensive one to leave running for nothing

* sample the headroom trace every 2s with a low-water mark instead of printing a spot value every 30s; shard 0 died 30s after a sample reading 13.8GB free, so the trace reported no pressure while saying nothing about the interval that actually killed the runner

* move the headroom trace out of the GHA action and into magus as internal/hostmem; a shell loop reading free/df worked on Linux runners and nowhere else and could not be tested without stubbing both, while magus already measures per-target peak RSS and had no way to say the machine itself was running out; Watch streams a warn line the moment available memory falls under an eighth of total, which is what survives a killed runner

* give a target a memory declaration and stream CI output; memory_mb rides the existing slot limiter rather than adding a second budget, because an author knows a race-enabled suite wants 10GB but cannot know how many slots that is on a machine they have never seen; declare the root test target at the measured 10GB and set MAGUS_LOG_STREAM in ci so a green run leaves a record of what it was doing

* name the running targets in the memory-pressure warning; the inflight registry already knows which target is executing and already survives a SIGKILL, so a warning that made the reader guess was withholding an answer magus had on hand

* fix the 13.1GB magusfile: stripGenerated grew a string with kept = kept + line over a 30,803-line coverage profile, and this VM's heap is append-only so every intermediate survived the run; joining a list once takes it to 29.5MB, and vm.HeapStats now reports the object count so the next one names itself instead of looking like a dead runner

* fix two defects the review found in the memory watchdog: heapstats.go referenced nanbox-only symbols from an untagged file and broke the buzz_safe and buzz_unsafe builds, and the low-water floor was never reset on recovery so a run that dipped, came back and then collapsed reported only the first dip; harden the darwin reader against a partial vm_stat; hold the shard ceiling at 2; cut the rationale that had been copied across five files down to one statement

* round the coverage badge to whole percent; hostmem and proc/run have files that compile on one platform only, so the ratio is 67.4 on macOS and 67.3 on a Linux runner, and committing a tenth of a percent made a generated artifact gate on the machine that produced it

* address the go-review pass: thread context through the host readers so a forked vm_stat cannot outlive its watchdog, cache the machine constant instead of forking per target, make slotsForPolicy pure, give LogMemoryPressure data and structured attrs so cache no longer imports the VM, join the watch goroutine and skip it on a dry run, record the heap peak before publishing the pointer, drop the peakrss bool nothing could observe, and name the units in TotalBytes, AvailableBytes and platform.memoryBytes

* attribute Buzz heap growth to a source position and rebase it per invocation; the interpreter samples every 4096 instructions so the allocator keeps its hot path, ReadHeapStats returns a struct rather than two same-typed ints, ResetHeapStats stops a daemon reporting the first run's peak against every later one, and pry gains a .heap command that ranks the lines filling the heap

* add a profiling guide, and make the drift gates say when they skip; the gate only fires from a clean tree, so any uncommitted work turned it into an exit-0 no-op that read as a pass, which is why drift kept reaching CI after a local run said it would not

* declare the gopherbuzz test target at its measured 12GB; the root package peaks at 13.5GB because this VM's heap is append-only and its tests run thousands of Buzz programs in one binary, so magus must keep lint and build off the machine while it runs

* add vm.HeapProfile and use it to find the real leak: the VM allocates a pinned heap slot per builtin method call, so a loop calling xs.append leaks one per iteration, which is the idiom the profiling guide recommends; a plan records the measurements and puts bound-method dispatch ahead of any collector

* stop allocating a pinned heap slot per builtin method call; OpInvoke's fallback arm went through getMember and got a fresh DirectValue every time, so a loop calling xs.append leaked one slot per iteration, and the inline cache now learns the directObj so a monomorphic site allocates nothing

* drop heap-attribution samples that carry no line number, and stop asserting a sampled ranking; a chunk without line data surfaced as <main>:? and outranked the real loop on a runner, and the test demanded the top slot for something probabilistic so it passed on macOS and failed on linux

* use the spellings typos accepts in the profiling guide; running lint locally carries the rw charm so format rewrote the file and the command passed, while ci strips rw and the same check failed
