Deterministic output refs (#17)

* derive output refs from the cache key; attempts carry execution identity

* explain ref differences: store key lines; add --meta and describe target --cache

* resolve refs across machines; sign every artifact member; add query output --publish

* fix a self-assignment and parallel-slice indexing flagged by lint

* separate signing domains; bind a manifest to its key; push after recording output

* use out-prefixed example ref ids in docs and the magus-run skill

* refresh the coverage badge

* pin the ref contracts: hit reuse, id shape, attempt uniqueness, legacy ids

* document deterministic output refs; the printed id changes width

* prove the ref recovery paths: colliding refs list candidates that resolve; descriptor reads refuse to invent an empty run; a miss names the stores consulted

* refresh the docs changelog mirror

* rename key lines to key inputs; the name states what they are rather than how they are serialized, and stops reading as 'the important lines'

* mark the two backward-compatibility shims with a compat(until:) note; record the convention and how it differs from Deprecated

* record that TODO and FIXME comments stay; godox is deliberately not enabled

* enable godoclint; fix the doc drift it found, including three comments naming a symbol that was renamed away and file headers Go was reading as second package docs

* enable gocheckcompilerdirectives; a malformed //go: line is a silent comment, and this tree leans on build tags in 52 files

* enable godot at declarations scope; punctuate the doc comments it found and move runnable commands into godoc code blocks where they read better anyway

* deny redirecting magus output, not just piping; lead each denial with the replacement it wants, and correct the claim that --tee captures console output

* deny running magus from a scratchpad copy of the workspace, and stop a trailing git advisory from downgrading a deny; carry the output and cwd rules to every provider surface, not just Claude

* remove the magus_scratchpad MCP tool; a disposable per-workspace file only ever encouraged agents to work outside the session, and the handoff journal covers what deserved to persist

* mirror the typed magus reports in the dry-run host; drop the goModReplace stubs the bindings retired

* spell it unparsable; the typos gate rejects the generated docs

* invert a missing output ref back to the target that would mint it; MGS8001 leads with the run command

* hoist tool versions and source expansion out of the ref sweep; the sweep drops from 35s to 1.4s

* stamp each descriptor with the revision its inputs were read at

* read default charms from the loaded config; state the recorded revision as provenance

* document predicting a ref that resolves nowhere; correct two pages calling a ref machine-local

* give magus_output the same finding; an agent pasting a CI ref hit the same dead end

* keep clihint a stdlib-only leaf; say when magus_run_target cannot reproduce a bare-charm ref

* open the observability test caches mutable explicitly; an immutable cache writes no entry

* clear the ambient cache policy before the cache package tests

* let doctor recognize the runtime env vars magus reads; they are not config fields

* unset rather than blank the env these tests isolate; t.Setenv leaves the key present

* print the inspect hint on CI too; document what the cache key includes and excludes

* fold the sweep-scoped reuses into one argument; share one ShortRevision; cover the untested branches

* upload a failing shard magus logs; the console drops thousands of lines and takes the assertion with it

* extract a semver from probe output instead of keying on it verbatim; bucket it by the spell's declared ranges; seed the cache key with the host platform

* key the host platform by default; let a spell claim its toolchain is platform independent and a target override it

* narrow a probed version with x/mod semver Major and MajorMinor; name the declaration VersionKey with an UpTo component enum

* let a spell declare its version keys and a target declare its platform sensitivity; reject an unknown component at decode instead of silently keying exactly

* add isValid, canonical, major, majorMinor and satisfies to the semver module as free functions; keep the existing three untouched

* make semver compare order three-way like every other library; move the relation form to satisfies; regenerate the langservice manifest, which had drifted to dot separators and predated the xml module

* let a version key opt out of extraction verbatim; key govulncheck on its whole output so a vulnerability database update still busts the cache

* stop guessing which number in a probe's output is the version; make extraction opt-in via upTo and declare it on the spells whose tools pad their version line

* mirror a named string type as a buzz enum instead of a bare str; unwrap an enum case when decoding a spell record; declare version keys through VersionComponent

* type a json patch op as PatchOpKind and mirror it as a buzz enum; unwrap an enum case in the generic value converter so every host read sees its backing value

* give the readiness and edge-direction fields real types instead of documenting their values in a trailing comment

* convert a defined type over a basic kind when crossing to buzz; a type switch matches identity, so every named-string field was arriving as null

* mirror the remaining named string types as buzz enums; generate Valid, Values and String from the same registry so a validator and its error text cannot drift from the cases

* rename the version contract functions to probe, matching the Go API and describe output; add a per-tool readiness probe so a stopped docker daemon fails as MGS3004 instead of a build failure

* generate Valid as a switch; scanning Values allocated a slice on every call to answer a predicate

* gate op dispatch on its tool's readiness probe; a stopped docker daemon now fails as MGS3004 before the op forks instead of surfacing as a build failure

* give a supervised service its own state type; print the platform policy in describe; list readiness gates in doctor without running them; document tool identity and the null fix

* replace cache.immutable with cache.write.enabled; split the platform key line into cache.include.os and cache.include.arch

* let a target override cache.include.os and cache.include.arch, nested the same way magus.yaml writes it

* wait up to 30s for a tool to become ready when a terminal is attached; fail at once without one

* carry a readiness probe's own output into MGS3004 and wrap the underlying failure instead of paraphrasing it

* document readiness probes and version keys in the spell-authoring contract table

* collapse five per-tool spell declarations into one mgs_getTools keyed by bin; drop the privileged primary probe so every tool keys as spell:tool:version

* let a tool declare the oldest version its ops work against; reject it as MGS3005 before the op forks instead of on an unrecognized flag

* cross every runtime boundary type in a test, with the list generated from the registry that emits the mirrors

* add magus doctor --probe to run declared readiness gates instead of only listing them

* drop the mgs shell_alias and the remaining pin comments from mise.toml

* magus run generate docs: add the MGS3004, MGS3005, and tool-identity entries active.urls.lock was missing

* fix the lint findings CI's real run surfaced: separate two doc-comment-bleed cases (moduledecls.go/types.go package-doc conflict; EdgeDirection/ServiceState swallowing the preceding type's comment), delete two functions the merge orphaned (readinessOf, decodeVersionKey - both fully superseded by logic already inlined elsewhere), fix an errorlint (%v -> %w), a forcetypeassert (checked cached.(error)), a missing test assertion (spell_test.go's failing-prober case never checked its error), and correct the floorVerdict probe-failure comment, which cited an MGS3003 diagnostic that does not actually exist anywhere on the spell-op dispatch path

* classify a missing tool binary as MGS3003 on the spell-op exec path (run.Exec and its unused twin run.Run), matching what std/os.go's os\which() already gives a Buzz script; fix internal/cache's TestMain, which was built specifically to isolate the package's tests from the ambient cache-policy env but still cleared the pre-rename MAGUS_CACHE_IMMUTABLE name, silently losing that isolation the moment cache.go started reading MAGUS_CACHE_WRITE_ENABLED instead

* drop the dead MAGUS_CACHE_IMMUTABLE fallback in cacheImmutable: cfg.Cache.WriteEnabled() already reflects it through the standard config-load path, so the raw env read was a no-op duplicate of the first check, not an independent guarantee; update the now-stale comments and warn message in build.go/store.go to name cache.write.enabled instead of the env var that superseded it

* shorten the PR advice preface; set it off as a blockquote from the findings below

* fix a real race in RunAll's dependency barrier: waitForDeps unblocked the instant an upstream's markDone channel closed, without checking whether the upstream had actually succeeded; markDone runs as a defer inside the SAME goroutine errgroup wraps, so it fires before errgroup observes the returned error and cancels the shared ctx, leaving a window where a dependent could see its failed upstream as merely done and run its own fn anyway; carry the upstream's error through markDone/waitForDeps directly so this no longer depends on ctx-cancellation timing; add a deterministic depBarrier-level test that reproduces the exact ordering every run instead of hoping a scheduler catches it

* pin a genuine --- FAIL:/panic:/FAIL test marker against budget eviction in failureExcerpt: internal/cache's own suite verifies magus's failure reporting, so it is unusually dense with EXPECTED lines like [fail]/cause: that match the same loose diagnosticLine regex a real failure does, and the real RunAll dependency-barrier race this session found was invisible in the CI console for exactly that reason - later incidental matches evicted the one real --- FAIL: block from a 24-line budget; add a regression test reproducing that shape verbatim, proven against the old code (fails outright without the fix)

* rank pinned failure lines inside the excerpt budget instead of exempting them; the exemption let a broadly-failing go test emit 30 lines against a 24-line cap while reporting omitted=0; fix the Immutable doc comments the env rename inverted; make waitForDeps prefer a settled upstream over a cancelled ctx so the dependency name is not a coin flip; drop the dead MAGUS_CACHE_MODE setenvs and stale comments

* make --step work: only the uncalled run.Run consulted the step gate, so the flag forced concurrency to 1, demanded a TTY, then ran everything without prompting; Exec consults it now and [c] latches in the gate rather than on a ctx copy that never reached the next command; classify a path-form missing binary as MGS3003 too, which arrives as ENOENT and not exec.ErrNotFound; delete run.Run, now redundant

* let a spell declare how its tool reports findings: Tool.diagnostics names a documented convention rather than a per-tool shape, so magus implements the standard once instead of carrying models of each tool's output that only that tool's maintainers can keep true; add a tolerant GNU parser tested against real hadolint and shellcheck output; declare it on hadolint, which carries -f gnu in the op's own args because magus never rewrites argv

* regenerate the gopherbuzz index; refresh the coverage badge to 67.5%

* give every workflow one trigger and one promise; fold publish-site and the image snapshot into cd and build both from source, un-breaking a docs deploy red since 2026-08-04; move compat to a nightly cron; rename cd.yaml to release.yaml

* document what belongs in a workflow and what belongs in a target

* replace the snapshot charm with cd plus a checked channel; stable and unstable each assert the version belongs there

* lowercase a whole initialism when mirroring a Go field, so ID stops crossing as iD; align the runtime encoder with the declaration

* let a magusfile raise a coded diagnostic; refuse the MGS prefix and mirror the shape as a Diagnostic record so a caught value narrows

* stop flagging security as a lint fragment; a scanner needs skip_cache, and folding it into lint would cost that phase its caching

* fix three British spellings the typos linter rejects

* add a recommendations page: the charm conventions magus promotes but does not enforce, and where it declines to recommend one

* regenerate the diagnostics index

* report a cancelled child as cancelled, not exit -1; a killed process reports ExitCode -1, so one real failure looked like several

* cut the docs render peak RSS with split/join; the replace loop interned a fresh copy of every page per occurrence

* upload a shard's magus logs when it is cancelled, not only when it fails; an OOM-killed runner took the only explanation with it

* regenerate the library indexes for the render change

* name the signal when a child is killed; SIGKILL means the OOM killer far more often than anything a reader would guess

* run CI under the gha charm so tool findings become inline annotations; advise when a run is on a CI provider whose reporter charm it never asked for

* regenerate for the signal-reporting and doctor changes

* record running targets on disk so a killed run is reported by the next one; one file per pid, host-scoped liveness, flush under one lock

* print the cache tier and name the projects in the run header; give RemoteBackend a Name so the header describes the backend without probing it

* move raise's cause and url into opts; url was unreachable without a cause because the trampoline binds by index; drop the CI-charm doctor check, which could never see a run's charms and so only ever advised

* discover tour steps from the directory instead of a hand-kept list, which had silently omitted four of them; add a step on charm axes and raising a coded diagnostic

* regenerate the library indexes

* use the shared json package in the inflight tracker

* put the affected base on its own header line; name the root project in the two drift messages that omitted it, which were the only ones not runnable from a subdirectory

* regenerate the library indexes
