give the docs url-ledger files a consistent .urls.lock extension: urls.lock -> active.urls.lock, retired-urls.txt -> retired.urls.lock; update the URL_LOCK/RETIRED_URLS constants + the abandoned-link hint string in render.buzz

-- 7a0656aac56a92eb2e0d0e8b4dbf36e3e8436d8c --
consolidate the static-site generator from website/ into docs/ and drop the scribe name; website/ stops existing - its SSG, templates, assets, package.json, and gen/ move INTO the top-level docs/ dir so docs/ is the ONE documentation project holding BOTH the markdown AND the renderer that emits it, co-located. Rename the generator off scribe: scribe.buzz -> docs/render.buzz, scribe_{dom,html,text,url,date,feed}.buzz -> docs/render_*.buzz, and every import/call site (magusfile import scribe -> render; render.site_render/writeSiteManifest/updateUrlLock). Fix the path model: from docs/ the ROOT/DOCS args still resolve (../docs == docs), OUT is now docs/gen; the magus.project sources watch the local markdown corpus + SSG inputs instead of ../docs. Guard the content walk + collectDocPages with a root-only generator-dir skip-set (node_modules/gen/src/tour) + a MAGUS.md file skip, so the walk never ingests the SSG's own subdirs as pages while nested content dirs named gen (docs/manpage/gen) still render. Sweep every remaining website/scribe reference across the repo: Go runtime paths (cmd/magus-utils/release.go served dir, magusfile build-playground wasm output, the mermaid-drift + tour test fixtures) and comments, the Pages workflow (docs/gen), README/CONTRIBUTING/CLAUDE/testing prose, the magus-vcs skill (source + re-stamped install), and retire the old /development/website/ catalog URLs. Verified: docs/render.buzz parses + its 40 in-file tests pass; describe lists a docs project (no website); a scratch render emits 175 pages with the exclusions holding (no node_modules/MAGUS/src/tour leakage, manpage pages intact); go build clean, go test 81 ok, graph verify up to date

-- 4281391e032fe92ba0a3e5b9b805ba9b8f8c7136 --
assemble the GitHub Pages artifact from both projects: the docs site at the root and the standalone console app under /console/, instead of uploading website/gen alone. A run step copies website/gen to the artifact root, then clears and replaces the /console/ subtree wholesale with console/gen - the console project OWNS /console/, so this discards the docs render's /console/ redirect stub (which exists only to satisfy the docs asset-integrity gate) and the app wins there. Trigger on console/gen/** too. Each project keeps its own committed, drift-gated gen/; nothing is duplicated into a gh-pages branch

-- ebd438855abc20834d8a95b0899d29a6843a6782 --
register the standalone console/ as a first-class magus project so magus affected ci covers it; add console/magusfile.buzz binding typecheck (tsc gate), test (node:test suite), build (pnpm esbuild -> gen/ with a drift gate, skip_cache + exclusive), lint (-> typecheck), and the ci anchor (typecheck + test + build). Mirrors the website project's pnpm + drift-gate pattern but simpler - no scribe, no proto codegen (the console vendors a committed src/gen client), no changelog/md-generate. The ts spell makes its TypeScript symbol-capable for the knowledge graph. Verified via a source-built magus: describe lists console among the projects; typecheck/test/build/ci all pass with no gen drift

-- c57f2a26b471759a38b9ed100d5464a61c3f12f7 --
stop the docs site rendering the console; the four surfaces (graph/logs/dashboard/activity) and the SPA moved to the standalone console/ project, so rip the moved-source references out of the docs generator. scribe.buzz: drop renderConsole/renderGraph/renderLogs/renderDashboard/renderConsoleShell/consoleRefLinks and their site_render calls + the per-surface CSS esbuild steps; the docs no longer emit /console/*. Delete website/{console,dashboard,graph,logs}.html and the build-{console,graph-explorer,log-viewer,dashboard,activity} pnpm scripts; drop the console-surface precache from sw.js.tmpl (keep console.css - the docs + playground still load it) and the console/* entries from pruneStaleOutputs' alwaysKeep. Point the nav + footer Console link and the PWA manifest Console shortcut at the SPA (/console/), and reduce the offline page to the playground. Move the loopback-API reference doc docs/console.md -> docs/reference/console.md (title Console API, aliases the old /console/ so a redirect stub keeps internal links resolving; the console project's deploy copy overwrites /console/ with the SPA); fix the README/knowledge/daemon/output-refs links that pointed at the old standalone surface URLs. Verified: scribe parses + its 40 in-file tests pass; a scratch-dir render emits the /console/ alias stub, the moved page at /reference/console/, and resolves every nav/footer/offline console link

-- 52dab645b9c9c642b496dd4bafcf6e518507a870 --
make the keybinding editor an integrated modal overlay instead of a full-page tab; a rebind dialog is something you summon over your work and dismiss, not a lens you park in a tab. Rebuild keybindings.ts as createKeybindingsOverlay in the command-palette family (centered box over a dimmed backdrop, Esc/backdrop-click to close, stops propagation so a chord being recorded never also fires its command); drop the PageModule/surface registration and open it from the Edit keybindings command; keep the pure keybindingRows + its test. Restyle #keybindings-overlay off the old #console-outlet surface selectors

-- 8b7b3eebf36ba6f37a71409352b617f6e994b092 --
fix the console tab strip collapsing to an unreadable sliver; the title bar is a semantic <header> so Pico gave it 1rem block padding, which under border-box ate the 2.2rem height down to a ~2px content box and clipped the tabs - zero the header margin/padding, raise the bar to 2.35rem, and bump resting-tab legibility (0.83rem, a readable blend toward the body color instead of the faint muted token)

-- 1acf913865ee80675d4c6134dc9f2212aed6d707 --
promote the command-graph tool grouping to its own `tool` kind (schema v3): a program (argv[0]) is an entity, not a command, so tool:<name> is distinct from the command:<...> invocation nodes; command --uses--> tool and, conveying the spell<->tool relationship, spell --uses--> tool, so kind:command holds only invocations, kind:tool lists the workspace's programs, and explain tool:go shows every command and spell that runs go

-- c2abe79a96a9d1c7ac87f33dd71fc348278d0f71 --
model a magus invocation as its full argv: replace journal.Command's Subcommand+Args with one Arguments []string (renamed from the inaccurate Verb; viewer proto regenerated); rename internal/cache output_store.go -> output.go (files only, type OutputStore unchanged); ingest test coverage as an observed @coverage overlay - coverage/covered_stmts/total_stmts on file+symbol nodes from the coverprofile magus already produces, plus a test_refs count from SCIP test-file references - so the graph answers which code lacks coverage; consolidate the duplicate path:line splitter to one splitPathLine; document graph query recipes (commands, refs path:line, coverage, insights) in docs/knowledge.md

-- a06101ac72365b5deaf8b72bbb77f08bacd95134 --
surface the definition file:line in `magus refs` from the symbol node's Source; replace the -o template field-list codegen with runtime reflection so bare -o template works for every output type (drop schema/gen/outputs.go and the magus-utils output subcommand); add a `command` knowledge-graph node kind (schema v2) minted from each target's evaluated dispatch plan - one node per concrete argv the workspace runs, with a language attr inherited from its spell, a command->uses->spell edge, and a base `command:tool:<tool>` grouping node that links every command running the same tool

-- a12dbee7c2b0b50faa5e204a7070d677982fbfdb --
add output field discovery and a generated output-type schema, and reduce daemon/worktree friction; render -o template over the -o json shape so template field names are the json keys; make bare -o template list an output's fields; generate schema/gen/outputs.go from the types/*Output structs via a new magus-utils output subcommand; classify a differently-built daemon's refusal as not-adopted (proc.NotAdopted, carried on the error) so forwarding to it runs locally quietly instead of warning; add daemon.enabled (--daemon-enabled, MAGUS_DAEMON_ENABLED, default true) to opt an invocation out of the shared daemon while keeping per-process recursion; consolidate proc's error vocabulary into errors.go and rename version/protocol skew to mismatch and remapWireError to decodeWireError; drop hardcoded drift/mirror tests (TestOutputsNotDrifted, TestSchemaNotDrifted, and TestKnownKeys's hardcoded key list) for the generate drift gate and property checks

-- 2a1b2317a873fe242003767260faac0a4c36e3e5 --
merge the console decoupling + native-console work into main

Brings the standalone console/ project (native PWA, own build, no scribe), the tiling/palette/keybinding work, and the four console surfaces. Website is intentionally WIP/broken mid-migration - see the HANDOFF BRIEF at the top of the console-native-shell-and-decouple-plan. Local only.

-- 62d69ee5babc3702a4a82c34ff88cbaa149a0335 --
make the decoupled console a self-contained native PWA that boots standalone (no scribe, no docs chrome). Author its own entry: index.html (a thin app shell - NO hat/brand, NO nav bar; the title bar IS the tab strip, and the settings gear FLOATS as a round button in the top-right corner, not inside a bar), manifest.webmanifest (standalone display + window-controls-overlay), and sw.js (precache the shell + bundles, loopback never cached). Rework surface mounting for the decoupled world: surfaces no longer lift a standalone docs page (there is none) - each owns a co-located scaffold.html (its <main>, extracted from the old website .html), which the build copies to gen/<surface>/ and standaloneSurface fetches. Full self-contained build (package.json): esbuild every bundle + all CSS + theme.js, copy the scaffolds + index/manifest/sw + vendored pico/favicons into gen/. console.css: title bar is tabs-only (2.2rem, no brand), the gear is a floating .console-fab pinned top-right with its popover, the tab strip reserves room so tabs never slide under it. Verified: the app boots from its own index.html on a fresh server, the log viewer mounts via the scaffold fetch, the gear floats, no hat; 0 typecheck errors, 70 tests pass. Remaining: tab-bar legibility + integrated keybindings (chrome polish), then website cleanup + reserve + the Pages copy-merge

-- f05707e10a743020055e1dff72769dd8c7c1ab30 --
decouple the console into its own top-level magus project (step 1: relocate + stand it up). Move website/src/console -> console/src/console (git-tracked renames, history preserved) and copy the shared deps it imports (lib/{daemon,persist,store,settings,glossary,refresh-toast}, ui/{console-settings,ref-drawer}, theme.ts, the magus + buf proto clients under gen/, and the console-wide styles) into console/src, mirroring website/src's layout so no import paths change. Add console/{package.json,tsconfig.json,.npmrc,.gitignore}: its OWN esbuild build (console.js + the four surface bundles + console.css), pnpm deps (protobuf/connect/connect-web/d3/uplot), and TS gate - fully independent of the docs site, no scribe. Drop the dashboard's standalone-only docs-chrome self-wiring (initNav/initSearch/initRefDrawer/initConsoleSettings) since it never boots a standalone page now. The new project typechecks (0 errors), its tests pass (70), and it builds all bundles standalone. WIP: website still references the moved source (scribe renderLogs/graph/dashboard/console + build scripts) and does not build until the next step (rip those out + reserve /console/); surface mounting, the hand-authored index.html/manifest/sw, and the native-PWA chrome redesign also follow

-- 0a2faa73b839d9d595b91d451f49045b929f25bb --
wire the native console page into scribe: renderConsole reads the hand-authored console.html, fills its <!-- settings --> with renderSettingsPanel(true) and <!-- csp --> with the default CSP, and writes gen/console/index.html - mirrors renderDashboard but wears NO renderConsoleShell (console.js builds its own title bar/tabs/status bar) and injects no root (console.html hard-codes ../ paths). It is called LATE in site_render (after the docs walk and writeAliases) so the SPA is the last writer of gen/console/index.html and definitively owns /console/: a pre-/console/-move docs artifact currently sits at that path, and calling renderConsole late means the app wins whether or not the walk re-emits it. Keep console/index.html + console/console.js + console/activity/activity.js from pruning; precache console.js + activity/activity.js in sw.js.tmpl (page HTML caches at runtime like the sibling console pages). Source only - no worktree generate (it bakes the branch into docs footers); scribe.buzz parses and its 40 in-file tests pass, and the rendered output was verified byte-equivalent by booting a harness built from console.html. On main: magus run generate writes gen/console/index.html + gen/sw.js and the console is reachable at /console/; the drift gate verifies it

-- 74f4bd5c2b2aa2966cbd15897ab0cf9319605502 --
add website/console.html: the hand-authored source page for the native console SPA. It follows the same convention as dashboard.html/graph.html/logs.html (a full head with the shared pico/theme/site/ui-panels/console stylesheets, PWA manifest, theme-color, and a <!-- csp --> placeholder scribe fills), but a THIN body: just the three slots the shell needs (#console-titlebar with the hat brand + #console-tabs + a #console-actions holding a <!-- settings --> placeholder for renderSettingsPanel(true), #console-outlet, and an EMPTY #console-statusbar) plus the console.js module script. console.js builds the tab strip, mounts a surface per tab/pane, and swaps the active tab's per-tab status bar into the footer, so the footer must stay empty (a static #console-conn/#console-demo/etc would duplicate the per-tab ids). Asset paths are ../ (the page renders to gen/console/index.html, one level under gen). Source only - the scribe renderConsole that processes this into gen/console/index.html, plus the SW precache/alwaysKeep entries for console.js and activity/activity.js, land on main where scribe can actually render (generate no-ops for HTML in a worktree); this page is inert until then. Verified by serving it at the real gen/console/index.html path: the console boots, the home launcher lists all four surfaces, and opening the log viewer lazily loads its bundle via the real ../ paths, no console errors

-- 648572610f86ca7ff2315380aad695fa1f4d4125 --
tear down surface lifetimes on console unmount, and stop the dashboard demo button from reloading the SPA: each mountable surface now exports deactivate() and standaloneSurface calls it before detaching the DOM, so closing a tab or pane leaves nothing streaming or ticking in the background - the log viewer aborts a live SSE stream, the dashboard latches transport.stop() and stops its demo interval, and the graph stops the force simulation (its rAF wobble was the main drain), aborts a live SSE + its reconnect timer, disconnects the stage ResizeObserver, and removes its window/document lifecycle listeners (routed through one AbortController). Separately, the dashboard 'See a demo' button called location.hash + location.reload(), which inside the console tore down the whole SPA; it now enters the showcase in place via beginDemo() and records #demo with replaceState (no reload, no hashchange) so a standalone refresh still lands in the demo. Verified in a harness: demo enters in place with both tabs intact (a window marker survived, panels revealed), closing the dashboard tab fired the interval clears, and reopening booted fresh; no console errors. The graph 'See a demo' button's reload is left as a follow-up (an in-place demo needs the async load pipeline replicated in the 2600-line canvas module)

-- 56a75a974d7597ba2b225a46686c46698a7d4341 --
add a keybinding editor surface: a table of the console's commands, each with its chord and Record / Clear / Reset controls, so a binding is rebindable in-app. It edits the ONE persisted keymap cell that installKeybindings and the palette read live, so a rebind takes effect immediately with no save step; Record captures the next keystroke in the capture phase (intercepting it before the global listener), Clear disables a binding (empty chord), Reset drops the override back to the default, and a red warning flags when two commands share a chord (via the existing conflicts()). It is a real PageModule (opens as a tab or a tiling pane, dogfooding the shell), built into the console bundle since it pulls no heavy deps, opened via the palette (console.settings.keybindings). keybindingRows is pure + unit-tested; the buttons use --pico-contrast not --pico-color (the bare-button dark-on-dark trap). Verified in a harness: rebind New tab to Cmd+N (persisted, and mod+n then opened a tab), a duplicate chord flagged both rows as conflicting, Reset restored the defaults and cleared the conflict, buttons legible in light and dark; no console errors

-- 655d2622308a9244f8bce891e199daa351186ddf --
add a command palette (mod+k) over the console command registry: one searchable overlay lists every registered command with its current chord, so an action is discoverable and runnable without memorizing a key. It is a pure view over listCommands + the merged keymap - a new palette.ts with matchCommands (a pure substring filter, unit-tested) and a thin overlay whose own keyboard handling (arrow to move, Enter to run, Esc to close, click-out to dismiss) stops propagation so it never fights the global listener; commands.ts gains formatChord (mod+shift+k -> Cmd+Shift+K on macOS, Ctrl elsewhere; unit-tested) and exports isMac for the label folding. main.ts instantiates the palette after registering the tab/pane commands and binds console.palette.open to mod+k. Verified in a harness: mod+k opens via the global listener, chords render per-platform, typing filters, arrows move the selection, Enter/click run the command and close, Escape closes; no console errors

-- b08102fca817fe3ad0d48e624d9e6d2131c5a606 --
wire split-pane tiling into the console: a tab's body is now a Pane tree rendered by tileView, so two surfaces sit side by side as lenses (a log next to the graph node it touched). tileView renders the tree as nested CSS grids with draggable dividers, mounts a surface per leaf, tracks the focused pane, and drives the pure tiling ops; a reconcile reuses cached pane/split elements by id so a re-render moves nodes rather than remounting untouched surfaces, and a divider drag updates only the dragged container's grid template (no reconcile) then persists the settled ratio on pointerup. An empty split pane shows an in-pane launcher (single-instance surfaces already in the tab are omitted); setLeafPage fills it. main.ts hosts one tile per tab: mod+backslash splits (auto axis via pickAxis), mod+shift+backslash splits down, alt+hjkl moves focus by nearest centroid, mod+w closes the focused pane and falls through to the tab when it was the last; the focused pane owns the shared per-tab status bar. The tab's tree serializes into the persisted workspace so a tiled layout restores on reload. Verified end to end in a throwaway harness: split dashboard-left/logs-right, drag the divider (ratio persisted), alt+h/alt+l focus, close-pane collapse, split-down stack, and reload-restores; no console errors

-- 9d8a028646a752f22d39181b773c57e40ba0de35 --
make alt-chords layout-stable in the keybinding engine: Alt+<letter> composes a dead key or symbol in e.key on many layouts (macOS Alt+h, Alt+l), so a written alt+h never matched; chordFromEvent now recovers the physical letter/digit from the layout-independent e.code whenever Alt is held, so the pane-focus bindings (alt+hjkl) fire reliably across platforms; KeyChord gains an optional code field, installKeybindings already forwards the real KeyboardEvent; non-alt chords are unchanged (the typed key still wins, so shifted symbols keep working); unit-tested

-- a60626b81844843ef78583010da5d68afd5259d6 --
carry a per-tab split-pane tree in the workspace model: TabState gains an optional layout (the tiling.ts Pane tree), absent for the common single-surface tab and present once a tab is tiled; a setLayout reducer records a tab's tree after a split, close-pane, or divider drag, replacing only the matching tab so it composes with the existing tab reducers and leaves the active tab and siblings untouched; serialized whole into the persisted workspace so a tiled layout restores on reload; pure and unit-tested

-- a910b511a53c7b40f710aee697d875c953668c1b --
add pickAxis and neighborInDirection to the tiling model: the two pure geometry helpers a split-pane UI needs, ported from Tack's studio (its v/h axes re-expressed as our row/col, its DOMRect candidates as a testable Rect subset); pickAxis reads a pane's aspect ratio to choose a split direction with no direction UI (wider splits to a row, taller to a col, walking a Fibonacci spiral), neighborInDirection finds the nearest-centroid pane in a screen direction for alt+hjkl focus moves; both pure and unit-tested over whole objects

-- 84ed7b37221c26aae2255f515d9d76f47250bb9a --
focus an already-open surface instead of opening a duplicate tab: logs/graph/dashboard/activity are single-instance (module-level state), so a second instance would fight the first - open() now activates the existing tab when the surface is already open; home stays multi-instance so the + affordance keeps spawning fresh launcher tabs; verified in a harness (reopening logs focuses it, + opens additional home tabs)

-- 35c4c84b85a9768ab910eff491818f50996ff084 --
add tab keybindings to the console: mod+t new tab, mod+w close tab, mod+alt+arrows for next/previous (Cmd on macOS, Ctrl elsewhere), registered as named commands on the existing command engine and layered over the user's persisted keymap so they are rebindable; centralize the workspace mutations in the console (the tab strip now only reports intent - onSelect/onClose - and the console owns activateTab/closeTabById/cycleTab), so a click and a keybinding drive the exact same ops and the strip re-renders off its ws binding; verified in a harness that new/close/next/prev fire and that click-to-switch and the close-X still work

-- 896b77de505dfb3ce23236e46a3f1afcd1dd40a0 --
give the console native chrome: a compact title bar and a per-tab status bar; make the log viewer full-bleed. The tab strip no longer borrows the standalone site-header (which read as a tall floating navbar) - it sits in a purpose-built 2.35rem #console-titlebar as [app mark | tabs fill | top-right actions], the actions holding the settings gear (theme + daemon host + poll, wired via initConsoleSettings + theme.js) so the corner buttons are back, and the active tab merges into the bar's bottom border so tabs read as anchored, not floating. The bottom status bar is now PER-TAB: the console builds one status bar per mounted tab (real elements, so the log viewer's zoom control and its listeners survive switches) and swaps only the active tab's into #console-statusbar, so switching tabs shows that tab's own status; a backgrounded streamer no longer leaks - the dashboard exports setVisible() and suppresses its shared #console-conn/#console-observing/#console-demo writes while hidden (its tiles keep updating), replaying state on return, wired through a new PageController.setVisible hook. Full-bleed: the nested-mount main gets a slim 0.4rem side gutter instead of Pico's ~1rem, so the log viewer stretches nearly edge-to-edge. Drop the unused stub CSS. Verified in a harness: compact anchored tabs, settings panel opens, per-tab status swaps with no cross-tab pollution, standalone dashboard unchanged

