-- 9b48b44d3b6637cb70822115f8c4b37070d1db34 --
add the console's own tiny reactive-DOM layer (console/view.ts): Signal interface (which persisted already implements), signal(), bind() (the one explicit reactive primitive - immediate + on-change, returns a disposer), scope() (collect disposers for teardown), and h() re-homed from card.ts (card re-exports it, tiles unchanged); unit-tested. Rebuild the tab strip on bind()+scope() as the reference use - persisted IS a Signal, so the view layer drives it directly. No dependency; primitives capped tight per the view-layer spec

-- 54e78560e18960e9a96c2c8d2b25086a79c6b66c --
ts spell: add tsc-build and tsc-clean ops

No build or clean op existed at all - a dead verb pair in TS projects (every
other language spell has both). tsc-build uses TypeScript's project-references
incremental build mode (tsc --build), which works even without declared
references via its own .tsbuildinfo cache, and declares dist/** as provided
output. tsc-clean mirrors it (tsc --build --clean), removing both the declared
outputs and the incremental cache. Add examples, extend the golden builtins
fixture, and add the two ops to the dry-run host's hand-maintained
builtinSpellOps manifest (internal/dry/manifest.go) - the playground/spell-docs
tracer's op allowlist, separate from the real registry since the wasm build
can't pull in the spell package's bytecode/codec.

-- 30f47e31b6c809ef4d25bed86d2635db84176163 --
ts spell: investigate opaque hashing vs node_modules; make tsconfig*.json explicit

Investigation (D4): mgs_isOpaque is informational only (types.WithOpaque's own
doc: "Informational only") - it does not change hashing at all. The actual
required-globs walk already excludes node_modules via internal/cache/hash.go's
isIgnoreDir (skips the directory by name during WalkDir, regardless of glob
breadth; covered by internal/cache/glob_test.go). The hypothesized node_modules
churn bug does not reproduce, so **/*.json is not narrowed. dist/coverage
aren't pruned by name, but the spell has no build op yet to populate them
(D6 will declare its outputs when it lands).

Make tsconfig*.json an explicit required-glob entry regardless, since today
it only matches by riding along on the broader **/*.json pattern.

-- a94d95256e090a5e9982a29f39aedc3c3fd7e6ae --
ts spell: fix required-glob cache-miss gaps; add eslint rw/gha charms

mgs_listRequiredGlobs listed .ts/.tsx/.js/.jsx but not the .mts/.cts/.mjs/.cjs
module-variant extensions mgs_listClaimedGlobs already claims, so editing one
of those files never marked the project affected; same gap for yarn.lock and
bun.lockb (only pnpm/npm lockfiles were required). eslint was also the only op
with no rw/gha charms, unlike golangci-lint and ruff-check; add --fix and
--format=unix (eslint has no built-in github formatter, unlike ruff). Update
the golden builtins fixture, charm-pinning test, and regenerate spell
bytecode and docs (also refreshes the other built-ins' bytecode, stale since
the skip_cache Target-field rename).

-- 7f5c8f19b68505c67a91108de8d37075192e3159 --
make the log viewer boot-deferrable so the shell can mount it: dom.ts resolves its handles in resolveDom() at boot instead of at import (the exports become live let bindings, so every importer is unchanged), keeping global getElementById so shared status-bar elements still resolve when mounted; main.ts wraps the boot in an exported activate() and auto-boots standalone only when the scaffold is already present, so the shell can inject a host then call activate(); standalone viewer verified unchanged (render, keybindings, zoom, switches)

-- 0aa09d83b0bfe92a26646964ababdad0091640a8 --
add MGS1003: warn on bespoke phase-fragment target names

A target named typecheck, type-check, vet, audit, security, style, or
prettify carves a subset out of an existing canonical phase (lint/format)
instead of composing into it, so it's invisible to any ci that only needs
lint - the check silently never rides along. New magus doctor check (warning,
not a load error; the name can stay if that's deliberate) plus its MGS1003
doc page.

-- 022f82b5f62d5a9b6758a47c351c00c7f4eba5bc --
add magus run/affected --no-cache

There was no one-shot rebuild flag; users had to magus clean --cache or
magus.bustCache. Add cache.Step.SkipReplay: unlike NoCache (never replay,
never snapshot), it skips the replay path for one run but still snapshots on
success, so the entry is refreshed rather than left stale for the next
ordinary run. Wire it through magus.WithNoCache into both `magus run` and
`magus affected` (flag parity test requires both); document in cache.md and
the run manpage source; regenerate api.lock.

-- 81fb6237406b1683f752d826ef26e99e2ad40b3e --
add the console shell orchestration (startShell): mounts one PageModule per open tab into a content outlet - each kept in the DOM and hidden when inactive so switching is instant, torn down on close - driven by the persisted Workspace and the tab strip; ships a real home launcher surface plus stubs for the not-yet-mounted apps; esbuild build-shell entry; verified open/switch/close/persist end-to-end

-- 9f37ef14f4cbc9f81a24aac7b9c5244e897162aa --
add the console shell's tab strip component: renders a Workspace (tabs.ts) as role=tab spans - dodging Pico's button/[role=button] theming - and drives the pure reducers on select/close/new through shell callbacks; subscribes to the persisted workspace so cross-tab and shell-driven changes reflect; pure tabViews mapping unit-tested

-- 9015be2effbefff46bd267a6fcd39dd39f99268b --
remove dead afterTarget/Step.After plumbing

stage.afterTarget was never assigned by any caller, making the Step.After
ordering edge unreachable from any user configuration - a leftover from the
era when RunCI hardcoded the pipeline chain. Delete the field, its dep_barrier
handling (checkAcyclic keeps working over DependsOn alone), and the two tests
that only existed to exercise the edge by hand-constructing Step.After
literals; fix the cache.md mention of an edge users could never create.

-- 20896a133b7e5e639bc8507de6fcbc2f7207288d --
rename skipCache to skip_cache; no legacy alias

magus.project's options map mixed conventions: depends_on and watch_ignore are
snake_case, skipCache was camelCase, in the same map. Hard rename across the
Go bindings (project_ns.go, dry/host.go), the generated Target Buzz mirror
(types.Target's json/buzz tags, regenerated via go generate), every magusfile
and tour source, and docs. The old spelling is now a loud unknown-key error
with a did-you-mean, per the A4 strictness this rename relies on - no
compatibility alias.

-- 4ba6ca7ac80141160db231cfce925f0254c4ba5c --
wire the graph explorer's actions to the command engine: focus-search / zoom-to-fit / toggle-layout become named commands bound to single keys (/ f l), dispatching to the existing controls and guarded against typing; overrides ride the shared persisted keymap, matching the log viewer

-- ac196cb7f3120d743c43961306a6efe9978c7768 --
race diagnostics must not be satisfied by a cache hit

magus run test --race built its cache step the same as any other, so a
cached target replayed and raceRT.TrackProject never wrapped it - the
detector observed nothing. Force NoCache when Race or RaceReplay is active;
fix volatility.md's wording (magus race diagnostics, not the toolchain
detector).

-- 8561ca5b4efde6f0df96a33e117ba959f574334f --
reject unknown keys and bad values in magus.project options

magus.project's top-level options map and its per-target policy table both
silently dropped unrecognized keys and malformed values: a typo like
depend_on or skip_cache vanished with no diagnostic, and a slots value that
was non-int or <= 0 was quietly ignored instead of erroring. Both maps now
reject unknown keys with a did-you-mean suggestion, and a present-but-invalid
slots value is a load error; a per-target policy naming a target the project
never declares (neither a custom export fun nor a spell op) is now also a
load error instead of silently producing a phantom "custom" entry in
`magus describe targets`. The dry/playground host mirrors the same strictness.

Running this against the repo's own magusfiles surfaced a real bug this was
built to catch: website/magusfile.buzz's "sources" option had never been
implemented, so its documented source globs were silently never tracked in
the project's cache key or affected set. Added it as a real option
(workspace.WithSources) rather than deleting the magusfile's intent.

-- d47ba63ecb25f4c692f20786b106bd3002a6747e --
unify needs-literal normalization with target registration

resolveTargetQuery's literal arm lowercased its pattern instead of running it
through DefaultTargetNameNormalizer, so magus.needs(magus.target.literal("goBuild"))
could not resolve a target declared go_build even though both normalize to the
same registered key everywhere else; route it through the same normalizer
targetMap registration uses

-- d240714b6059b13efbee83b0382c4fefa7dff92f --
normalize target names at the run boundary

types.ParseTarget now runs the target through DefaultTargetNameNormalizer after
validation, so go-build/go_build/goBuild all resolve to one cache key and one
policy entry; workspace.WithTarget and the dry-run mirror normalize
declaration-side policy keys the same way, so a policy declared under any
spelling applies regardless of invocation spelling

-- 066490377f6c2b8405c4dc0d41eb39fdbeb45154 --
add a zoom control to the log viewer: a -/percent/+ control in the status bar and =/-/0 keys scale the view via a --log-zoom variable (text size in the log view, a magnified waterfall in timeline); level persists and clamps 70-220%; the control is spans not buttons so Pico's [role=button] theming never applies, and margin:0 keeps its div from inflating the status bar via Pico's default block margin

-- 225dc57836819abd4cf004d715be3fac1e2d169a --
replace the log viewer's relabeling Raw/Pretty and Timeline/Log toggle buttons with segmented switches: a recessed track holds a raised sliding thumb over the active side (built by makeSegToggle, JS-enhanced, equal-width segments); labels stay the muted toolbar color at normal weight, the thumb alone marks the active side; the visuals live on an inner track span so Pico's button-state coloring never bleeds through

